prince edward county about two hours  east of toronto  there is world class  in our backyard and always has been and  shining a light on that in  kind of a tragic awful time for our  industry  has been kind of that blessing in  disguise after five years of renovations  the royal pickton hotel opened weeks ago  amid ever-changing covid restrictions  it's designed with staycationers in mind  i really believe it will help kick start  that tourism and hospitality economy the  hospitality industry says it may provide  a 30 boost to revenue but it took 22  months to get into this hole it'll be at  least another 22 months with support to  come out of it  and gaviola global news toronto  ahead in cell ideology how the dangerous  subculture is targeting the next  generation of extremists  virtual school and pandemic restrictions  have meant more screen time for many  young people across this country and  experts fear that could make them more  vulnerable to so-called incels men who  blame women and social standards for  their involuntary celibacy  as abigail beeman explains there are  concerns incels are targeting the next  generation of extremists online  it's an innocent enough search term  self-esteem for guys but type that into  youtube and within four clicks when  global news tried we were offered all  kinds of posts promoting hate including  one video about embracing the idea of  violence in a society that despises what  it means to be a man it's very  frightening how quickly  a young man who might be lonely or might  be troubled or might be seeking  information  can have  disinformation  and hateful ideology put in front of him  experts say it's a new trend in cell  communities targeting not just teenagers  but even boys online we've had several  people  ages of between the ages of 10 to 12  years old pandemic restrictions have  meant young people spending more time  online amplifying radicalization says  this expert who runs a de-radicalization  program for young canadian men and boys  in terms of recruitment strategy it's  called get them young when they're young  and indoctrinate them into these  ideologies  incels a concept still relatively new to  most canadians many learning about it  for the first time in the wake of the  2018 toronto van attack that left 11  people dead including nine women now  portals of overlapping hate misogyny  white supremacy the far right aren't  hard to find on social media chat rooms  and gaming platforms i think we are  collectively coming to that point where  we are asking you know if not demanding  that social media companies really do  rethink those algorithms  experts say there's no easy solution but  there is a push for social media giants  to evolve algorithms and to remove and  disincentivize extremist content not  allowing creators to make money through  such sites you can actually see the way  in which these algorithms on platforms  start putting more and more radicalizing  content in front of in front of young  men who may have gone simply seeking  information about self-esteem or dating  or fitness or weight lifting in a  statement youtube says it has taken  additional steps to ensure that those  who aim to spread violent extremist  ideology cannot do so although the  company did not respond to any specific  questions about incels  meta which operates facebook and  instagram tells global news it acts on  in-cell related content that breaks  platform rules though not everything is  clear cut and they're now working with  experts to better understand the  environment there's also the legislation  option both the united states and europe  are looking at regulations targeting  algorithms canada has promised new bills  aimed at social media with stiff  penalties if companies don't remove  online hate but the heritage minister's  office wouldn't comment on specifics or  exactly what comes next abigail beeman  global news ottawa  next the problem with plastic and the  push for a global treaty to reduce it  we are surrounded by plastic whether we  see it or not according to a new report  on the effects of plastic waste it's in  our food our air our water and if action  isn't taken soon on a global coordinated  scale activists warn plastic pollution  threatens the future of life on this  planet in much the same way as climate  change redmond shannon reports  the enduro river in kenya hidden beneath  a blanket of plastic bottles it is not  me who was shocked you know i think it  is the boys who are shocked to see the  rivers so polluted they couldn't see the  water i couldn't see the water in the  river on a plastic water activist james  wikibia has seen it all when it comes to  plastic waste his country banned  single-use plastic bags five years ago  with some success canada is looking to  do the same with bags and some other  single-use items like straws yeah indeed  the whole world is addicted to the  convenience of plastic bags and that is  where the problem is  i  don't think  people will die when they stop using  plastic bags in fact there are growing  concerns that if we don't cut back on  making plastic our health and the  planet's entire ecosystem could be at  risk plastics and their chemicals are  they're in the air they're in our food  they're in our water they're in our  honey i found it on the highest mountain  peaks and the deepest deepest ocean  trenches i mean it quite literally is is  everywhere a new report from the ngo the  environmental investigation agency  highlights in particular microplastics  broken down from larger items the  invisible particles make their way into  our food chain chemical additives in  some plastics have been linked to birth  defects and cancers the reality is that  in order to meet the scale of the crisis  at hand we can't just have individual  actions and we can't just have  isolated fragmented national policies  that target this and that and all  different in different countries at the  end of next month the united nations  environment assembly begins in kenya  with hopes for a binding treaty this  month more than 70 large companies that  sell plastics including coca-cola and  unilever called for an agreement that  reduces plastic production as well as  improving waste management likened and  linked to the issue of carbon emissions  solving this problem will require  fundamental changes to how we live our  lives redmond shannon global news london  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm sophie louie tonight  your canada is this elk patiently  waiting to cross the street in canmore  alberta we would love to see your corner  of the country so please keep sending  your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching and have a great  night  [Music]
 

